sux-deployment_preface.sh|41|23|Deployment|||normal|||L1Sectionbody|        complete SolidGroundUX releases.|sux-deployment_preface.sh:::Deployment:
sux-deployment_preface.sh|42|24|Deployment|||normal|||L1Sectionbody||sux-deployment_preface.sh:::Deployment:
sux-deployment_preface.sh|43|25|Deployment|||normal|||L1Sectionbody|The release manager is deliberately self-sufficient. It always carries a small|sux-deployment_preface.sh:::Deployment:
sux-deployment_preface.sh|44|26|Deployment|||normal|||L1Sectionbody|standalone UI and default-theme fallback so it can bootstrap a clean machine or|sux-deployment_preface.sh:::Deployment:
sux-deployment_preface.sh|45|27|Deployment|||normal|||L1Sectionbody|recover a damaged installation. When a healthy SolidGroundUX framework is available|sux-deployment_preface.sh:::Deployment:
sux-deployment_preface.sh|46|28|Deployment|||normal|||L1Sectionbody|at the selected target root, the manager may reuse the normal framework UI primitives|sux-deployment_preface.sh:::Deployment:
sux-deployment_preface.sh|47|29|Deployment|||normal|||L1Sectionbody|and active theme without making the release engine dependent on them.|sux-deployment_preface.sh:::Deployment:
